THESIS IMPORTANT DOCUMENT
EEG + VISION + KINOVA ROBOTIC ARM EXPERIMENT
================================================================================

This document summarizes the complete experiment, system architecture, data
collection protocol, preprocessing pipeline, model training parameters, real-time
control logic, hardware/software environment, and important implementation notes.

Project root:
  C:\Users\Shivans1\Desktop\Kinova_arm_project\EEG_Kinova_Project

Main experiment goal:
  The goal of the experiment is to evaluate EEG-based motor-imagery control of a
  Kinova Gen3 robotic arm, and to compare EEG-only control against an EEG + vision
  hybrid mode. In the hybrid mode, EEG determines the intended movement direction,
  while OpenCV vision tracking modulates the movement speed based on whether the
  vision-estimated direction matches the EEG-predicted direction.

================================================================================
1. EXPERIMENT SUMMARY
================================================================================

Number of subjects:
  10 subjects

Task:
  Two-class motor imagery task.

Classes:
  CSV class label 1 -> Up / Right Arm imagery
  CSV class label 2 -> Down / Left Arm imagery

After label encoding:
  Model class 0 -> Up / Right
  Model class 1 -> Down / Left

Robot/OpenCV sign convention:
  Class 0 -> negative Cartesian X velocity -> up direction on screen
  Class 1 -> positive Cartesian X velocity -> down direction on screen

Experimental modes:
  1. EEG-only mode
     - Uses kinova_eeg_controller.py
     - EEG prediction controls arm movement without camera input.
     - Current controller uses position-based target switching with go_to_angles().

  2. EEG + Vision mode
     - Uses kinova_eeg_opencv_controller.py
     - EEG controls movement direction.
     - Vision/OpenCV controls movement speed magnitude and match/mismatch scaling.
     - If EEG and OpenCV direction match: robot moves at full speed.
     - If EEG and OpenCV direction mismatch: robot moves at half speed.

  3. OpenCV standalone mode
     - Uses OpenCV/track_ball_kinova_test_fixed.py
     - Vision-only ball/bar tracking reference implementation.

